00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.309 align:start position:0%
there's an unfortunate trade-off when it  comes to the high price of prescription  meds in this country the only way to  bring down branded drug prices for many  people is to give fewer choices despite  huge political uproar campaign speeches  and congressional hearings pharmacy  benefit managers or PBMs like CVS  Caremark and Express Scripts are the  only ones who have really managed to get  drug prices down but PBMs are no white  knights here's how they do it  they're basically middlemen they sit  between your insurer and drug makers and  negotiate discounts one PBM tactic is to  do deals with drug makers that may for  example give them the biggest discount  and then refuse to cover similar drugs  made by competitors in 2012 there were  just 34 drugs that CVS won't cover at  the beginning of this year there were  124 and even if drugs aren't formally  excluded it can still be difficult for  patients getting advanced and often more  expensive treatments because they're not  sticking up or they haven't tried a  cheaper medicine first meanwhile critics  say companies like CVS and Express  Scripts reap big profits when they  successfully negotiate drug prices if  they negotiate a price of an $80 pill  down to $40 a chunk of those savings  often in the form of a rebate can go to  the PBM the firms say they give much of  the savings back to clients but how much  of those savings is tough to tell the  size of the rebates on individual drugs  and how much PBM is actually keep are  one of the American healthcare systems  biggest secrets as drug prices skyrocket  higher there's room for bigger rebates  which may mean bigger profits PBM  revenue at CVS has grown to 100 billion  dollars since it entered the business in  a big way almost a decade ago by buying  Caremark PBM czar a uniquely American  invention in Europe some governments  negotiate prices they also restrict  medicines to bring down prices but the  difference is European governments  aren't making money off the negotiations  it's possible that PBM exists to take  advantage of a broken US health care  system
 

